# Deschooling is Attractive to Parents - But Is It Good for Kids? 

date: 06/20/26

I get the sense that many adults who investigate or pursue self-directed learning (SDL) for their children are adults who would, if they could, spend all day learning (like me) or creating (like my husband). The idea of spending 8 hours a day, surrounded by friends (or at least peers) getting the opportunity to explore whatever interest strikes my fancy, with at least some access to resources to do so, is the dream from the perspective of an adult who has been pigeonholed into a career, has chores and adult responsibilities, etc. 

But while SDL is certainly great for the right adult, can it really be true that it's great for every kid, and at every age, as the founders of the Sudbury School and other SDL pioneers believe? 

This question is important to me, because I think there are major, debilitating flaws in our education system. In fact, I believe that in the next 1-2 generations, public schools will look completely different than they do today. 

I don't want Ronan in large classrooms with teachers who do not have the time, resources, or ability to individualize his education. I do want him to retain the social norms and develop the peer networks to succeed in whatever he chooses to do with his time. I don't want him subjected to black and white rules and thinking that serves administrators and the broader school district instead of the individual child. But I do think it's important to learn certain things that a child might not choose on their own: reading, writing, math through Algebra, which preserves the option for students to pursue higher level math, biology, and US and World History, as well as significant physical, language, and arts and music education of their choosing.

Why do I think these specific subjects are important?

Reading, writing, and speaking: As Magaluzzi so aptly put, children express themselves in hundred of ways, and should be encouraged to do so. But in our society, reading, writing, and speech are the primary ways that we do so. I look at Ronan as a 13 month old, and he is relatively happy. When he is unhappy, it's generally because he can't express a need, or can't understand why his need is not being met. He can't fully communicate. In my opinion, the earlier we enable a child to communicate, the better.

A note on speaking: that's not generally a class you see. But speaking is at this point likely even more important than writing to other's perception of you at any professional level. I grew up thinking I was a terrible public speaker; I actually just needed to learn to speak. This should be part of any curriculum. 

Math through Algebra I. Algebra I is the gateway to all other forms of math. If a child makes it this far and hates math, fine, but at least you have been given the keys to the kingdom if you ever want to pick them up. That said, I agree that while worksheets can support math concepts, math is best learned in the real world.

Science through biology. Biology explains the building blocks of our bodies -of ourselves! As I've grown and learned more about how my own body works (fertility), I am shocked by how little I understood about my own bodily processes. We have great insight into our physical form, and children should learn it. I agree that Chemistry and Physics are quite specialized, and there's no need for every child to pursue. 

US and World History: Understanding basic history unlocks references in literature, but it also unlocks context for how humans behave, and the ability to see patterns in social movements, etc. 

To be clear, you can't "force" a kid to learn anything. But to not offer these core subjects unless pursued by a child is, in my opinion, reckless. 

The trope of the older and wiser elder is based in reality. As we age, we become more reflective and are able to see the broader view. Why deprive children of our collective wisdom? 

I also find it bizarre to allow kids to vote out staff members. And to not share anything beyond surface level with parents. 

But there are many things I love:

No artificial bell schedules. Deep work should be encouraged. And there's not necessarily any reason to learn every subject at the same time, or spread subjects across many years. If a student is really into math, why not let them get as far as they can? If they want to spend an entire semester writing and acting a play, I'm good with that. But to leave school without learning these building blocks is not okay.

Screen time needs to be regulated. That's the antithesis of self-directed learning. These devices and the apps on them are engineered by adults in order to maintain attention for as long as possible. 